anticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"The Nazi concentration camp system was shaped by changing political, legal, social, economic, and military forces, encompassing the experiences of both perpetrators and victims.","confidence":0.91},"academicOrientation":{"score":67,"confidence":0.88},"profileConfidence":0.9,"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"d67dbcdda9d9e489a8ca8ab5fc866e6a8647a0c8c9d05b628840fceeb242462f","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"History","secondarySubjects":["War & Military"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book provides a comprehensive historical account of the Nazi concentration camps, effectively situating it within the broader context of World War II and the Holocaust.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"b73b98c4a7dbe70f980fea58bf9ad4639934050425bc879c04f05177affb0add","summary":"The \"deeply researched, groundbreaking\" first comprehensive history of the Nazi concentration camps (Adam Kirsch, The New Yorker). In a landmark work of history, Nikolaus Wachsmann offers an unprecedented, integrated account of the Nazi concentration camps from their inception in 1933 through their demise, seventy years ago, in the spring of 1945. The Third Reich has been studied in more depth than virtually any other period in history, and yet until now there has been no history of the camp system that tells the full story of its broad development and the everyday experiences of its inhabitants, both perpetrators and victims, and all those living in what Primo Levi called \"the gray zone.\" In KL, Wachsmann fills this glaring gap in our understanding. He not only synthesizes a new generation of scholarly work, much of it untranslated and unknown outside of Germany, but also presents startling revelations, based on many years of archival research, about the functioning and scope of the camp system. Closely examining life and death inside the camps, and adopting a wider lens to show how the camp system was shaped by changing political, legal, social, economic, and military forces, Wachsmann produces a unified picture of the Nazi regime and its camps that we have never seen before.","pageCount":637,"displaySummary":"The \"deeply researched, groundbreaking\" first comprehensive history of the Nazi concentration camps (Adam Kirsch, The New Yorker). In a landmark work of history, Nikolaus Wachsmann offers an unprecedented, integrated account of the Nazi concentration camps from their inception in 1933 through their demise, seventy years ago, in the spring of 1945. The Third Reich has been studied in more depth than virtually any other period in history, and yet until now there has been no history of the camp system that tells the full story of its broad development and the everyday experiences of its inhabitants, both perpetrators and victims, and all those living in what Primo Levi called \"the gray zone.\" In KL, Wachsmann fills this glaring gap in our understanding. He not only synthesizes a new generation of scholarly work, much of it untranslated and unknown outside of Germany, but also presents startling revelations, based on many years of archival research, about the functioning and scope of the camp system. Closely examining life and death inside the camps, and adopting a wider lens to show how the camp system was shaped by changing political, legal, social, economic, and military forces, Wachsmann produces a unified picture of the Nazi regime and its camps that we have never seen before.","primaryTopic":"World War II","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"History","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"History","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"History","mappedSubject":"History","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book provides a comprehensive historical account of the Nazi concentrati